-- title:
--   Finiteness of F_N^{full} over ℚ(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero. Write $\mathrm{jq}$ for the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ as a Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ \mathbb{Q}$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ the set `divisorExpansions N` of all series $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}\, d\ \mathrm{jq}$ for nonzero divisors $d \mid N$, that is, the series obtained from $\mathrm{jq}$ by substituting $q \mapsto q^{d}$. Let `jFull N` denote the element of $F$ whose underlying Laurent series is $\mathrm{jq}$ itself (membership coming from the inclusion of the level-one modular function field in $F$). The theorem asserts that $F$, regarded as a module over the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}\bigl(\,\mathrm{jFull}\ N\,\bigr)$ obtained by adjoining the single element `jFull N` to $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $F$, is finite-dimensional; equivalently, $[F : \mathbb{Q}(j)] < \infty$. No further hypotheses on $N$ are imposed.
--
--   This is the classical finiteness of the modular function field of level $N$ generated by the $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$, over the rational function field $\mathbb{Q}(j)$. It is the arithmetic input used downstream in the construction of the Igusa scheme, where finiteness over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$ feeds the finite-type and ramification statements for the chart rings.
